WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.109
On the fixtures, AWS gives
five notices: two retirements,

2
00:00:04.109 --> 00:00:09.138
a maintenance window, a security
notification and a closed incident.

3
00:00:09.138 --> 00:00:10.300
Three need action.

4
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:11.538
Look at the basis.

5
00:00:11.538 --> 00:00:16.264
The EC2 instance retirement and
the IAM security notification are

6
00:00:16.264 --> 00:00:20.737
action required because AWS said
so, in its actionability field.

7
00:00:20.737 --> 00:00:24.565
The Lambda lifecycle event is
action required by the kit's rule:

8
00:00:24.565 --> 00:00:29.201
a scheduled change whose event code names
a retirement, due at its start time.

9
00:00:29.201 --> 00:00:33.788
Again, the record tells you which is
AWS's word and which is the kit's.

10
00:00:33.788 --> 00:00:38.503
And the kit holds AWS's documented
limit: the details call takes one

11
00:00:38.503 --> 00:00:43.476
to ten event identifiers, so eleven
is refused before anything is sent.

12
00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:46.204
Beyond monitoring and
receiving advisories,

13
00:00:46.204 --> 00:00:50.800
this gate supports implementing directives
in the established time frames:

14
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.240
an action-required event becomes
a task with its due date.
